. *******************************************************************
. * Attrition
. *******************************************************************
. 
. * Table 4: Sample attrition across the waves, as counts of children in each w
> ave. The DID only uses 2005 and 2009 and the event studies use all three wave
> s. The continuous DID uses the same samples, so this covers it too
. * Each row starts from the children who meet the regression sample rules in t
> he first wave and could stay in the sample, not from all children measured. T
> hen the later columns count who is still in the sample
. 
. preserve

. * Age cutoffs of the samples, same as in Build_DID_Analysis_Data.do
. local cut_haz 16

. local cut_waz 11

. local cut_bmiz 16

. 
. * The loop copies each child's age in 2002, 2005 and 2009 onto all of that ch
> ild's rows. That way, each row can compare ages across waves, for example age
> 2009-age2005. A missing age2009 marks the children whose 2009 age has to be f
> illed in some other way: from their birth date and the 2009 interview date, o
> r failing that, from the median gap.
. foreach y in 2002 2005 2009 {
  2.     gen tmp = age if year == `y'
  3.     bysort pid_link_canonical: egen age`y' = max(tmp)
  4.     drop tmp
  5. }
(79,682 missing values generated)
(25,805 missing values generated)
(83,548 missing values generated)
(34,007 missing values generated)
(79,721 missing values generated)
(35,117 missing values generated)

. 
. * Median age gap between the waves, for children under 16 measured in both
. * We use the median bc a few gaps are weird, like −4 or 44, probably from dat
> a entry errors
. gen gap0509 = age2009 - age2005 if year == 2005 & age < 16
(104,025 missing values generated)

. quietly summarize gap0509, detail

. local gap0509 = r(p50)

. local n0509 = r(N)

. * Share of these children within a year of the median, for the notes section
. quietly count if inrange(gap0509, `gap0509' - 1, `gap0509' + 1)

. local sh0509 : display %3.0f 100 * r(N) / `n0509'

. local sh0509 = strtrim("`sh0509'")

. gen gap0209 = age2009 - age2002 if year == 2002 & age < 16
(103,098 missing values generated)

. quietly summarize gap0209, detail

. local gap0209 = r(p50)

. local n0209 = r(N)

. quietly count if inrange(gap0209, `gap0209' - 1, `gap0209' + 1)

. local sh0209 : display %3.0f 100 * r(N) / `n0209'

. local sh0209 = strtrim("`sh0209'")

. 
. * Copy each child's 2009 interview date to all of their rows
. gen tmp = int_date if year == 2009
(67,103 missing values generated)

. bysort pid_link_canonical: egen int2009 = max(tmp)
(9,356 missing values generated)

. drop tmp

. 
. * This code below has essentially three steps
. * 1) Measured: if the child was measured in 2009, use that real age.
. * 2) Computed: if they're in MxFLS-3 but weren't measured, compute their age 
> from their birth date and their household's 2009 interview date. This only ha
> ppens if the birth date passed the cleaning check. This code is similar to th
> e cleaning script
. * 3) Estimated: if they're not in MxFLS-3 (or the birth date failed the check
> ), take their last measured age plus the median gap: 4 years from 2005, 8 fro
> m 2002.
. gen age_next = age2009
(35,117 missing values generated)

. replace age_next = floor((int2009 - mdy(bmonth, 15, byear)) / 365.25) if miss
> ing(age_next) & !missing(age_days)
(9,876 real changes made)

. replace age_next = age2005 + `gap0509' if missing(age_next)
(9,261 real changes made)

. replace age_next = age2002 + `gap0209' if missing(age_next)
(7,519 real changes made)

. 
